cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyundi (동현디): a single-player pencil-sketch blood glucose education game for type 1 diabetes in mg/dL, with the starting stage and the paper colour set through the esoteric language Donghyun-lang"
type: software
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24522934"
version: "0.02"
date-released: "2026-11-25"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Donghyundi is a browser-based, noncommercial educational toy for children
  and adults with type 1 diabetes and their families. The player looks
  after a few hours of a child's day and keeps blood glucose within
  70-180 mg/dL. The idea was suggested by Professor Sukdong Yoo of Pusan
  National University School of Medicine, whom the author thanks. Its
  game mechanic follows Level One: A Diabetes Game
  (Level Ex, now Relevate Health Games): food arrives on a conveyor belt,
  an insulin button sets and injects a dose, a juice
  button treats lows, time can be fast-forwarded, glucose, insulin and
  ketones are shown as particles inside the body, and each stage is scored
  with one to three stars. Unlike the original it uses mg/dL, opens a dose
  ruler with 0.5-unit marks (time stands still while it is open) instead
  of a hold-to-dial button, can save and load a game, runs in a
  tall 240x420 view meant for phones, is written in Korean, puts the
  tutorial on the page instead of inside the game, and is drawn by
  code as wobbly graphite strokes on paper using the pencil engine of
  Donghyuns. The one image file is the title on the briefing screen,
  written by hand by the author. Twelve stages teach, in order: a first injection, pre-meal
  timing, the insulin-to-carbohydrate ratio, estimating carbohydrate
  without labels, correction doses, insulin on board, hypoglycaemia and
  juice, exercise, ketones, sick days, ratios that change with the time of
  day, and unannounced snacks. Ten foods follow the one-exchange units of
  the Korean Diabetes Association food exchange lists. Target range, time
  in range, hypoglycaemia thresholds, pre-meal timing, dose arithmetic,
  sick-day and exercise rules follow ISPAD Clinical Practice Consensus
  Guidelines (2022, 2024) and American Diabetes Association patient
  guidance; the time course of insulin and food, the size of the exercise,
  illness and ketone effects, and the fail and star criteria are
  simplifying assumptions made for the game and are listed as such on the
  page and in the README. The starting stage (1-12) and the paper colour
  (rough cream or sketchbook white) are chosen through a program written
  in the esoteric programming language Donghyun-lang. Input is mouse or
  touch, with fullscreen. Clearing all twelve stages is celebrated with
  falling petals and a short fanfare synthesised in the browser from the
  trio melody of Elgar's Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1 (1901, public
  domain); no recording is used. No graphics, sound, text, stage data or
  code of Level One or any other game is used, and the project is not
  affiliated with or endorsed by its makers. It is not a medical device
  and must not be used to decide real insulin doses. The MIT license
  applies to the Donghyundi code and documentation; the page fonts are
  under the SIL Open Font License. See LICENSE and CREDITS.md.
keywords:
  - type 1 diabetes
  - diabetes education
  - blood glucose
  - serious game
  - procedural drawing
  - esoteric programming language
  - JavaScript
